    "details": {
      "biography": {
        "value": "<p><strong>Master of the <em>Kestrel Wife</em>, a fat Keoish wool trader out of Gradsul.</strong> Fifty-one. Non-combatant, and knows it.</p><p>The module gave him one line and it is the best characterisation in the chapter: a master who <em>put in twice at ports nobody told you about, because he is a trader before he is anybody's courier</em>. He is not corrupt. He is a small businessman with a hull, and the difference between those two things is why the party gets boarded.</p><p>He does not want to know what is in their bales. He has taken a councilman's agent's money for passage and letters and a story about wool, and he has decided the story is true. When the boarding proves otherwise he does not blame them and does not ask \u2014 he simply stops putting in at ports nobody told them about.</p><p><strong>On the quay at Gradsul, about fire:</strong> <em>&ldquo;No fire on my deck. Not for anything. Not if we're sinking, not if we're boarded, not if your own hair's alight \u2014 you come to me and I'll find you a bucket. I've seen a wool hull go up. It took four minutes and there was nothing left to bury.&rdquo;</em></p><p><strong>After the boarding, if pushed:</strong> <em>&ldquo;I've left money on a wharf in Highport that I'll not see again, and that's my business settled with you.&rdquo;</em></p><p><strong>Day thirty-nine, unprompted:</strong> <em>&ldquo;I'll not ask. But when you're ashore, and you're doing whatever it is \u2014 there's eleven of us on this hull and one of them's dead. I'd like it to have been for something.&rdquo;</em></p>",
        "public": ""
      },
